<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chemistry_2e_(OpenStax)/04:_Stoichiometry_of_Chemical_Reactions/4.02:_Classifying_Chemical_Reactions" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B5.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-414620 editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{5}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-414620">In basic solution, molecular chlorine, Cl<sub>2</sub>, reacts with hydroxide ions, OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, to yield chloride ions, Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>. and chlorate ions, ClO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>. HINT: This is a <em data-effect="italics">disproportionation reaction</em> in which the element chlorine is both oxidized and reduced. Write a balanced equation for this reaction.</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-414620">\[\ce{3Cl2(aq) + 6OH^{&minus;}(aq) -&gt; 5Cl^{&minus;}(aq) + ClO3^{&minus;}(aq) + 3H2O(l)} \nonumber \]</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
